# What Wrangler uploads as static assets, when assets.directory is "." (see
# wrangler.jsonc). This has NO relation to .gitignore -- Wrangler walks the
# real filesystem after clone, not git's tracked-file list, so anything on
# disk gets served unless named here. Confirmed by the build log for commit
# 16179ab: it uploaded .git/HEAD, .git/objects/*, .git/config and .git/hooks/*
# as live static assets, because nothing told it not to.
#
# Cloudflare's own migration guide (developers.cloudflare.com/workers/
# static-assets/migration-guides/migrate-from-pages/) lists what Pages used
# to exclude automatically and Workers does not: node_modules, .DS_Store,
# .git. A list like that only protects what someone thought to name -- which
# is exactly how .git ended up served in the first place. This repo is a
# single flat HTML app by design (README: "One HTML file. No build step"),
# so the safer shape is an allow-list: deny everything, then name only the
# 11 root files the running app and service worker actually fetch by path.
# A future top-level addition -- another script, another doc -- is excluded
# by default instead of leaking until someone remembers to list it.
#
# The 11 come straight from grepping the current files, not from memory:
#   index.html's own <head>            -> config.js, manifest.json, icon.svg
#   index.html's LEGAL_LINKS map       -> pricing/terms/refund/privacy/contact.html
#   sw.js's own SHELL cache list       -> ./, index.html, config.js, manifest.json, icon.svg
#   index.html's serviceWorker.register -> sw.js
#   the five legal pages' own <link>   -> legal.css
*
!/index.html
!/config.js
!/manifest.json
!/icon.svg
!/sw.js
!/pricing.html
!/terms.html
!/refund.html
!/privacy.html
!/contact.html
!/legal.css
